A tap, a drain and the work between them (@wu-observe: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #source "../sources/Manual-de-Saneamento-Funasa.lingua#r_65G5YY3QVCA8HAXMPC8RTPKEWE", #source "../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD", #water-and-sanitation "../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1") { r_4BZY7VTHAE6B6WH956HKPZY906
**Read:** [Manual de Saneamento — Funasa, fifth edition](../sources/Manual-de-Saneamento-Funasa.lingua#r_65G5YY3QVCA8HAXMPC8RTPKEWE), the water-supply and sewerage system descriptions; [Water safety plan manual — WHO and IWA, second edition](../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD), the system-description and operational responsibilities material.

A functioning tap depends on a source, intake, treatment where required, energy, storage, distribution, building plumbing and someone noticing when a part stops working. A drain begins another chain: collection, transport, treatment, residual handling and a destination. Water moves through both chains while information, money, equipment and skilled work cross their boundaries.

Choose a household, campus or industrial site. Draw the known path and mark what remains unknown. Separate direct observations from a utility map, a staff explanation and an assumption. Identify who controls the source, treatment, delivery point, wastewater connection and receiving environment. Include independent wells, tanks, onsite sanitation or reuse where they actually exist.

Follow one interruption backwards: loss of electrical supply can stop a pump, alter pressure and prevent treatment even when water remains at the source. Explain which service was lost, for whom, and what evidence would show restoration. This account will become more precise as quantities and processes acquire meaning.

